    "result_text": "The full build succeeds with all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the disposable-domain email blocklist by following the codebase's existing granular, one-concern-per-class validation pattern (mirroring `EmailNormalizer`/`InvalidEmailException`, `PostcodeValidator`/`InvalidPostcodeException`, etc.):\n\n1. **`DisposableEmailDomainValidator`** \u2014 a small static utility with a single responsibility: reject an already-normalized email whose domain is on the blocklist (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`). It accepts `null` (email is optional). I kept this separate from `EmailNormalizer` because that class owns *syntactic* normalization, whereas this is a distinct *policy* check on a syntactically-valid address.\n\n2. **`DisposableEmailDomainException`** \u2014 thrown by the validator, matching the existing exception convention.\n\n3. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice.handleDisposableEmailDomainException`** \u2014 maps the exception to `400 Bad Request` with an `errors: [\"email\"]` body, identical in shape to the sibling email/telephone/postcode handlers.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 one added line invoking the validator right after `EmailNormalizer.normalize(...)`, so it runs on the canonical lower-cased address.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsDisposableDomain` passes and the full suite is green.",
